    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §353, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S2405\n\nSPONSOR: KAPLAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the executive law, in relation to the listing of county\nand city veterans' service agencies\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nRequired the New York State Division of Veterans' Affairs to post on\ntheir website a listing of the local veterans' service agencies with the\nname, location, hours of operation and contact information for each\ncounty and city veterans' service agency in the State of New York. The\ndivision shall also provide and update this information in its annual\nreport to the governor and the Legislature annually.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends Section 353 of the executive law by adding a new\nsubdivision 24.\n\nSection 2: Effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nVeterans service on the local level began before 1887 when care of\nveterans and their families was administered by the G.A..R. (Grand Army\nof the Republic). As early as 1887, New York State law provided for care\nof veterans. In 1945, Article 17 Section 353 was enacted to establish\nCounty Veterans' Service Agencies. Directors and staff must be know-\nledgeable about laws and benefits on the federal, state and local level\nso that they are able to assist veterans and family members who seek\nhelp in navigating on how to obtain benefits that the veteran or family\nmember may be eligible to receive.\n\nBy requiring the posting of this information on the Division of Veter-\nans' Affairs website, it will make it easier for veterans and family\nmembers to locate these offices.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA. 11024 Passed Assembly in 2018.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nMinimal.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
